Sval: HSTS and site integrity keep every jar's userContextId

Each workspace jar and each private window is a jar of its own, told apart
by its userContextId. The site security and site integrity stores drop
userContextId from their keys, so an HSTS header one jar saw would upgrade
loads in every other, which a site can read back as an identifier that
links the jars. Every entry keeps it. The site security service follows
clear-origin-attributes-data, so a jar's HSTS entries go when its data is
cleared while the others stay; ClearDataService's HSTS cleaner has no
per-attributes delete, and nothing outside the service can list its
entries. The site integrity service clears its own the same way. No chrome
API reaches the storage keys.

